From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Roos <thomas@exein.io>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 15:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ollama: retry setup when the server is unreachable

If the Ollama server is not reachable at the moment Home Assistant starts,
the config entry fails permanently instead of being retried, and the
integration stays dead until someone reloads it by hand.

async_setup_entry() probes the server with client.list() and converts the
expected failures into ConfigEntryNotReady, which is what puts the entry
into the "setup_retry" state and gets it retried with backoff:

    except (TimeoutError, httpx.ConnectError) as err:
        raise ConfigEntryNotReady(err) from err

The httpx.ConnectError arm never fires. The ollama client does not let
httpx exceptions out - _client.py's _request_raw() catches them first and
re-raises the *builtin* ConnectionError:

    except httpx.ConnectError:
        raise ConnectionError(CONNECTION_ERROR_MESSAGE) from None

and httpx.ConnectError is not a subclass of the builtin ConnectionError
(it derives from httpx.TransportError -> httpx.HTTPError -> Exception).
So a connection refusal escapes async_setup_entry() as an unexpected
exception, the entry lands in "setup_error" rather than "setup_retry",
and Home Assistant never tries again.

Observed on this system: Home Assistant started while the machine running
Ollama was down. The log showed exactly one

    ERROR ... Error setting up entry http://<host>:11434 for ollama
    ConnectionError: Failed to connect to Ollama. Please check that
    Ollama is downloaded, running and accessible.

and no retry for the next 25 hours, long after Ollama was healthy again.
The damage is not limited to Ollama: because the entry never set up, the
conversation.ollama_conversation entity was never created, and the Assist
pipeline that names it as its conversation_engine then raised

    ValueError: Agent conversation.ollama_conversation not found

on every single request - there is no fallback to the built-in agent, so
the entire voice/text assistant was down, not just the LLM.

Catch the builtin ConnectionError, which is what the client actually
raises. Also widen httpx.ConnectError to its base httpx.HTTPError so
transport failures on any code path that does not go through
_request_raw() (httpx.ConnectTimeout is likewise not a TimeoutError
subclass) are covered too. Both are strict supersets of what was caught
before, so no previously-retried failure stops being retried.

ollama.ResponseError is deliberately *not* included: it is the conversion
of httpx.HTTPStatusError, so it means we did reach an HTTP server that
answered with an error, and a permanent API-level error should surface as
a failure rather than retry forever.

Still present upstream as of the 2026-05-12 dev branch; the tuple has not
been touched since the integration was added in #113962. Unlike the tibber
patch this one has no known expiry - drop it once upstream fixes it.
---
 homeassistant/components/ollama/__init__.py | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/homeassistant/components/ollama/__init__.py b/homeassistant/components/ollama/__init__.py
--- a/homeassistant/components/ollama/__init__.py
+++ b/homeassistant/components/ollama/__init__.py
@@ -66,7 +66,7 @@ async def async_setup_entry(hass: HomeAssistant, entry: OllamaConfigEntry) -> bo
     try:
         async with asyncio.timeout(DEFAULT_TIMEOUT):
             await client.list()
-    except (TimeoutError, httpx.ConnectError) as err:
+    except (TimeoutError, ConnectionError, httpx.HTTPError) as err:
         raise ConfigEntryNotReady(err) from err
 
     entry.runtime_data = client
